<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthago Nova</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 227 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Cartagena, New Carthage, Qart Hadasht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthago Nova, modern Cartagena in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was founded around 227 BCE by the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> as the capital of Barcid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, with a fine harbour and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> captured it in 209 BCE. It became a leading Roman city, the capital of the province of Carthaginiensis, and later a Byzantine stronghold.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthago-nova.jpg" alt="Carthago Nova" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nanosanchez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_romano_cartagena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The newly conquered lands of Gallaecia, Asturia and Cantabria were attached to Citerior, probably during Augustus' visit of 16–13 BCE, creating a vast province that stretched from the Pyrenees and the Mediterranean coast to the Atlantic shores of Galicia and south to <b>Carthago Nova</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They took wine and olives from the Greeks, horse breeding mattered greatly to the nobility, and they worked the silver mines near Gadir and <b>Carthago Nova</b>, iron in the Ebro valley, and tin and copper.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in about 229 BCE, his son-in-law Hasdrubal extended Carthaginian power by diplomacy and founded <b>Qart Hadasht</b>, later Carthago Nova, around 227 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li><li><blockquote>Spania was run by a magister militum based at <b>Carthago Nova</b>, and an inscription of 589 records the governor Comenciolus repairing the city's defences.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzantine-spania">Byzantine Spania</a></li><li><blockquote>It took in the south-east coast and much of the central Meseta, including Toletum, with <b>Carthago Nova</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/carthaginiensis">Carthaginiensis</a></li><li><blockquote>After Carthaginian control under Barcid Iberia, the region passed to Rome when Scipio captured <b>Carthago Nova</b> in 209 BCE, and was pacified by 197 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/contestani">Contestani</a></li></ul></section>
